<div class="body">

  <!-- Core Argument Framing -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="core-arg">
      <div class="core-arg-label">The Case for This Research</div>
      <div class="core-arg-title">HCI, AI, and Cybersecurity Are Inseparable, and Organizations Cannot Afford to Treat Them as If They Are Not</div>
      <div class="core-arg-body">
        <p>Human-computer interaction, artificial intelligence, and cybersecurity are not three separate fields that happen to overlap. They are three dimensions of the same experience. Every time a person interacts with an AI-powered system, they are simultaneously navigating an interface shaped by HCI principles, making decisions with security consequences, and engaging with technology that is learning from and about them. These three things are happening at once, in every interaction, for every user.</p>
        <p>Organizations that build or deploy AI-powered technology, whether they are developing their own AI products, integrating third-party AI tools into their services, or using AI internally, are making decisions that sit at the intersection of all three. When they treat HCI, AI, and cybersecurity as separate concerns to be handled by separate teams at separate stages of development, the result is systems with gaps. Those gaps are where users get confused, where security fails, where trust erodes, and where the people least equipped to protect themselves get hurt most.</p>
        <p>This is not a theoretical concern. AI companies are in the news for lawsuits. Governments are passing emergency legislation. Vulnerable populations, elderly users, people with disabilities, children and teenagers, those with mental health conditions, low-literacy users, and others, are bearing consequences that organizations did not anticipate because they did not study them. Society as a whole is navigating an AI landscape that is moving faster than the guidance available to the people building it.</p>
        <p>This research applies existing knowledge across HCI, AI, and cybersecurity to address that gap. Its audience is broad: businesses and organizations of every size, from startups to established enterprises, that are building or deploying AI-powered technology; technology and cybersecurity professionals navigating these intersections daily; policymakers setting the rules that govern them; and society as a whole, which is already living inside the systems these decisions produce. Vulnerable populations are a critical focus of this research because they are where the consequences of getting the intersection wrong are most severe and least studied. But the research does not stop there. Every organization operating at this intersection needs to understand it, because it affects their daily operations, their external products, their internal tools, their legal obligations, and the people they serve, whether those people are considered vulnerable or not.</p>
        <p>What makes this research both urgent and necessarily forward-looking is the pace at which this landscape continues to change. AI capabilities that were experimental two years ago are now embedded in consumer products used by billions. Regulatory frameworks enacted last year are already struggling to keep pace with what organizations are building and deploying today. New threat vectors are emerging faster than protective guidance can address them. The digital society this dissertation examines is not a fixed state - it is an evolving one, and the organizations, professionals, and populations navigating it are doing so in real time, without the benefit of fully settled answers. Any framework built for this landscape must account for that reality, offering not just guidance for the current moment but a foundation for continued understanding as the technology, the risks, and the policy environment all continue to evolve. That is the orientation of this research: applied and grounded in what is known today, and structured to remain relevant as what is known continues to grow.</p>
      </div>
