        <!-- HCI Card -->
        <div id="vc-hci" class="venn-card" style="background:rgba(25,10,45,0.97);border:1px solid rgba(168,85,247,0.5);">
          <button class="venn-card-close" onclick="closeVennCard('vc-hci')">x</button>
          <div class="venn-card-title" style="color:rgba(168,85,247,1);">HCI: Human-Computer Interaction</div>
          <div class="venn-card-body">Human-computer interaction is the discipline that studies and designs how people engage with technology. It draws on psychology, design, cognitive science, and engineering to understand not just what users do, but why they do it, how they make decisions, where they struggle, and what kinds of interfaces and systems make technology more understandable, usable, and trustworthy. In the context of this research, HCI is the lens through which AI and cybersecurity must be examined, because both depend entirely on human behavior to succeed or fail. A security system that users cannot understand will not be used correctly. An AI system that users cannot trust will not be adopted responsibly. HCI is not a layer added on top of technology design. It is the foundation from which all of this research proceeds.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- AI Card -->
        <div id="vc-ai" class="venn-card" style="background:rgba(35,28,10,0.97);border:1px solid rgba(207,185,145,0.5);">
          <button class="venn-card-close" onclick="closeVennCard('vc-ai')">x</button>
          <div class="venn-card-title" style="color:rgba(207,185,145,1);">AI: Artificial Intelligence</div>
          <div class="venn-card-body">Artificial intelligence refers to computer systems designed to perform tasks that typically require human intelligence, by learning from data rather than following rigid hand-written rules. In this research, AI is examined as a technology that changes, complicates, and amplifies the human-technology relationship in ways no prior technology has. It is a tool that can improve accessibility, automate decisions, and extend the reach of services. It is also a threat vector, being actively used to generate synthetic imagery, clone voices, impersonate trusted individuals, and scale social engineering attacks in ways that were previously impossible. And it is infrastructure, now embedded in the platforms, services, and systems that billions of people interact with daily, often without knowing it. Understanding AI as all three of these things simultaneously is central to the applied research this dissertation pursues.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Cybersecurity Card -->
        <div id="vc-cyber" class="venn-card" style="background:rgba(20,10,35,0.97);border:1px solid rgba(192,132,252,0.5);">
          <button class="venn-card-close" onclick="closeVennCard('vc-cyber')">x</button>
          <div class="venn-card-title" style="color:rgba(192,132,252,1);">Cybersecurity</div>
          <div class="venn-card-body">Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting systems, networks, and data from digital attacks, unauthorized access, and harm. In the context of this research, cybersecurity is understood as a fundamentally human discipline, not merely a technical one. The majority of security incidents involve a human element: someone clicking a phishing link, ignoring a warning, using a weak password, or failing to respond to an alert in time. AI is reshaping this landscape, making threats more sophisticated and more personalized, while also making security systems more complex and harder for everyday users to navigate. The intersection of cybersecurity with HCI and AI is where this research lives: examining how human experience, design decisions, and AI-powered systems interact to either protect or expose the people who depend on them.</div>

        </div>
