          <div class="venn-card-title" style="color:rgba(251,191,36,0.95);">Organizations of Every Kind</div>
          <div class="venn-card-body">From AI startups to global enterprises, NGOs to government agencies, any organization building, deploying, or using AI-powered technology is operating at the intersection of HCI, AI, and cybersecurity, whether they recognize it or not. This includes startups and AI companies building products from scratch, whose foundational design and security decisions will shape every user interaction the company ever has. It includes established businesses integrating AI into existing products, services, and internal operations. It includes large enterprises and AI companies whose reach means that getting this intersection wrong has consequences at scale, as documented cases of lawsuits and regulatory action make clear. And it includes NGOs and government agencies whose obligation to the people they serve is highest precisely where those people are most vulnerable. The intersection affects their daily workflows, their compliance obligations, their products, and the experience of every person who touches what they build.</div>
        </div>

        <div id="vc-vp" class="venn-card" style="background:rgba(50,12,22,0.97);border:1px solid rgba(255,100,120,0.4);">
          <button class="venn-card-close" onclick="closeVennCard('vc-vp')">×</button>
          <div class="venn-card-title" style="color:rgba(255,100,120,0.95);">Vulnerable Populations: Critical Focus</div>
          <div class="venn-card-body">Elderly users, people with disabilities, children and teenagers, those with mental health conditions, low-literacy users, and others whose needs fall outside the assumptions embedded in most technology design. These are the people where the consequences of getting this intersection wrong are most severe and least studied. They are not the only people affected by how organizations navigate HCI, AI, and cybersecurity. But they are the people most severely affected when it goes wrong, and the least represented in the research and design processes that shape these systems. Designing with them in mind produces systems that work better for everyone. That is what makes their inclusion in this research strategically necessary, not just morally right. Vulnerable populations are also not a single category, they are intersecting identities. A person can be elderly and have a disability. A teenager can have ADHD and low digital literacy. These intersections compound risk in ways that no single intervention can address, and that existing research has not fully examined.</div>
