  <!-- Research Topic -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-hdr"><span class="sec-title">Research Topic</span></div>
    <div class="topic-block">
      <div class="topic-label">Final Dissertation Title</div>
      <div class="topic-title">Human-Computer Interaction, AI, and Cybersecurity: An Applied Integrated Framework for Risk, Trust, and Responsible Technology in an Evolving Digital Society</div>
    </div>
    <div class="sentence-block">
      <div class="sentence-label">Research Identity Statement</div>
      <div class="sentence-text">"I research the intersection of human-computer interaction, AI, and cybersecurity as an integrated and inseparable whole, examining how this intersection affects people, organizations, and society at large. This research carries two interconnected focuses: the organizations, businesses, NGOs, and government agencies of every size, that build, deploy, and implement AI-powered technology, and who must understand this intersection to operate responsibly, protect the people they serve, and navigate the legal, ethical, and reputational risks that come with getting it wrong; and the broader population those systems affect, with particular attention to vulnerable populations who bear the greatest consequences when the intersection is not understood or designed for. The goal is to draw on existing research across HCI, AI, and cybersecurity to give organizations practical, applied guidance they can sustain as these technologies continue to evolve and reshape daily life across every sector of society."</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- How It All Layers Together -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-hdr"><span class="sec-title">How It All Layers Together</span></div>
    <div class="three-circles">
      <div class="circle-card">
        <div class="circle-num">1</div>
        <div class="circle-label">The Interconnected Foundation</div>
        <div class="circle-text">HCI, AI, and cybersecurity cannot be separated. How do people interact with technology where all three are at work simultaneously - and what happens when they do not understand, trust, or safely navigate that intersection?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="circle-card">
        <div class="circle-num">2</div>
        <div class="circle-label">The Complication</div>
        <div class="circle-text">AI changes everything at that intersection - making systems more powerful, more opaque, and higher stakes. It is also being actively weaponized against the people those systems are supposed to serve.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="circle-card">
        <div class="circle-num">3</div>
        <div class="circle-label">The Organizational Responsibility</div>
        <div class="circle-text">Who is most harmed when organizations get this intersection wrong - and what does existing research tell us that organizations, technology and cybersecurity professionals, and policymakers must understand and do differently?</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Core Research Questions -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-hdr"><span class="sec-title">Core Research Questions</span></div>
    <div class="bullet-card">
      <div class="bullet-card-hdr">What This Research Asks</div>
      <div class="bullet-list">
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How do people interact with technology, AI, and cybersecurity - and do they actually understand, trust, and navigate them safely?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How does AI change the human relationship with technology and cybersecurity - and does it make that relationship better or worse?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>Do AI-driven security interfaces improve or worsen human decision-making during security incidents?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>What UX design principles make AI-powered technology and cybersecurity tools more usable and effective for everyday users?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>What are the policy and ethical implications of AI making autonomous security decisions?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How can UX design of AI security tools be leveraged to guide legally compliant user behavior?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How do people form, calibrate, and lose trust in AI systems - and what design, behavioral, and psychological factors drive that?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How do people relate to AI not just as a tool but as something they interact with, depend on, and are affected by emotionally and cognitively?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>Are AI-powered technology and cybersecurity systems designed in ways that vulnerable populations can actually use, understand, and safely interact with - and what are the legal and ethical implications when they are not?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How do businesses, NGOs, and government agencies go about building and implementing AI-powered technology and cybersecurity, and what does understanding the humanistic approach - including its legal, ethical, and human-centered dimensions - require of them in practice?</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>How are generative AI tools being used to harm individuals - through synthetic imagery, voice cloning, sextortion, and AI-enhanced fraud - and what does that mean for design, policy, and the protection of vulnerable populations?</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Vulnerable Populations -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-hdr"><span class="sec-title">Vulnerable Populations: A Critical Focus Within the Broader Research</span></div>
    <div class="bullet-card">
      <div class="bullet-card-hdr">Populations at Greatest Risk When the Intersection Is Not Understood</div>
      <div class="bullet-list">
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Elderly users</strong> - May struggle with AI-generated security prompts, unfamiliar interfaces, and complex consent language. May over-trust AI due to unfamiliarity with its limitations. Are disproportionately targeted by cybercrime - including romance scams, grandparent fraud, and AI-enhanced impersonation schemes - and are increasingly targeted by AI-powered versions of these attacks that are harder to detect and easier to fall for. Cognitive decline further compounds all of these risks and intersects directly with disability (see below).</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>People with disabilities</strong> - Disability is not a single category. It encompasses a wide range of physical, sensory, cognitive, and neurological differences that each carry distinct implications for how a person interacts with technology, cybersecurity systems, and AI. Key disability types and their intersections with technology risk include: <em>Visual impairments</em> - screen reader users may encounter AI interfaces that are not accessible, miss visual security indicators, or be unable to verify URLs and sender identity through standard visual cues; <em>Hearing impairments</em> - audio-based security alerts, voice authentication systems, and AI voice interfaces create systematic exclusion and missed warnings; <em>Cognitive and intellectual disabilities</em> - difficulty parsing complex interfaces, multi-step authentication flows, legal consent language, and security warnings that are designed without plain-language accessibility in mind; <em>Neurological differences (ADHD, autism spectrum)</em> - different patterns of attention, trust, and social inference that AI and security systems rarely account for, and that can lead to both over-trust and under-trust in ways that create exploitable vulnerability; <em>Motor disabilities</em> - reliance on alternative input methods (voice control, switch access, eye tracking) can expose sensitive information in shared or public environments; <em>Cognitive decline and acquired disabilities</em> - progressive conditions like Alzheimer's and dementia create layered vulnerability that worsens over time, often intersecting with aging to compound risk in ways that are not well understood in either the HCI or cybersecurity literature.</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>The intersectionality of vulnerability - why categories cannot be treated as separate</strong> - These populations are not mutually exclusive, and treating them as separate categories misrepresents how vulnerability actually works. A person can be elderly and have a visual impairment. A teenager can have ADHD and low digital literacy. A woman targeted by NCII may also have a mental health condition that shapes how she experiences and responds to the harm. An elderly person with early-stage dementia interacting with a romance scam chatbot is not experiencing one risk factor - they are experiencing compounding, intersecting vulnerabilities that no single intervention can address. The research must account for these intersections explicitly, because the people in those overlapping spaces are often the most harmed and the least represented in existing scholarship.</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Low-literacy users</strong> - Cannot adequately parse legal disclosures, security warnings, or AI-driven alerts. Cannot advocate for themselves in AI-mediated systems. This category often intersects with cognitive disability, limited education access, and language barriers in ways that compound the underlying literacy gap.</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Users with mental health conditions</strong> - Anxiety, depression, paranoia, PTSD, ADHD, and other conditions fundamentally alter how users perceive, trust, and respond to AI and technology systems. Poorly designed systems can cause real harm and compound existing challenges. Mental health conditions also intersect with disability in both clinical and legal definitions, and the research must address that overlap rather than siloing them.</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Low digital literacy users</strong> - Cannot evaluate AI outputs or recognize AI limitations. May not understand they are interacting with AI at all. Foundational technology barriers precede every AI and security barrier on top of them. Digital literacy gaps intersect heavily with age, disability, education access, and socioeconomic status.</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Children and teenagers</strong> - The first generation growing up with AI as ambient infrastructure. They interact with algorithmic systems before they have the cognitive development or digital literacy to understand them. They are subject to data collection from childhood, face unique risks around AI-generated content, manipulation, and exploitation, and are often the most emotionally and developmentally impacted by AI-mediated social environments - yet are among the least represented in AI design and safety research. Children with disabilities or developmental differences face compounded risks in this space.</div>
        <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Those required to interact with technology as a whole</strong> - Cybersecurity is inseparable from technology. Every security layer built on top of a system a user already struggles to navigate becomes an additional barrier and risk. These users cannot opt out of systems that have become essential infrastructure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Real World Problems -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="sec-hdr"><span class="sec-title">Real World Problems This Research Addresses</span></div>
    <div class="sentence-block" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
      <div class="sentence-label">Why These Problems Are Connected</div>
      <div class="sentence-text">Every problem below exists because HCI, AI, and cybersecurity are being treated as separate concerns. When organizations understand the intersection of all three - and apply that understanding to how they build, deploy, and govern technology - these problems become addressable. That is the applied contribution this research works toward.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">1</div><div class="rw-title">Are AI-powered technologies truly accessible - and when they are not, do they exclude people or introduce the very risks they were meant to prevent?</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">As AI-powered technology becomes embedded in banking, healthcare, government services, social media, workplace automation, and the growing range of platforms integrating AI, a core question emerges: are these systems genuinely accessible to everyone, or is accessibility a compliance checkbox rather than a design commitment? When security and authentication are built without the full range of users in mind, vulnerable populations may face barriers that result in exclusion, or worse, systems that introduce more risk than they prevent.

      A subtler question also arises: when does trying too hard to be secure, or too hard to be accessible, itself become the problem? Over-layering security can produce fatigue and workarounds. Removing friction in the name of accessibility can strip away protections that were actually serving a purpose. This research asks where the line is between security that protects and security that harms, and between accessibility that includes and accessibility that exposes.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">2</div><div class="rw-title">AI is making decisions that humans do not understand</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">As AI becomes more deeply integrated into the systems people depend on, questions arise about how autonomous decision-making affects the humans on the receiving end of those decisions. When an AI-driven system makes a determination that impacts someone's access to services, financial accounts, or personal data, there is reason to examine whether people can understand what happened, why it happened, and what - if anything - they can do about it. The degree to which AI decision-making is transparent, contestable, and legible to everyday users is not yet fully understood, particularly across different populations and contexts. This research asks what happens to trust, agency, and safety when people interact with systems that make consequential decisions they may not be able to interpret or challenge - and what design and communication standards might make those interactions more equitable and accountable.</div>
    </div>


    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">3</div><div class="rw-title">AI is built by humans - and human biases can be embedded in the systems it powers</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">AI systems learn from data generated by humans, and the people who build them bring their own perspectives and blind spots to the process. This raises a well-documented concern: that biases in training data, design decisions, or implementation choices become embedded in AI outputs in ways that are difficult to detect, contest, or correct. When AI tools are used in hiring, lending, healthcare, legal proceedings, or content moderation, biased outputs do not affect all users equally. They tend to concentrate harm in communities already underrepresented in the data that shaped the system. This research asks how awareness of bias should inform how organizations design, evaluate, and deploy AI-powered technology, and what a human-centered implementation framework must require of those who build it.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">4</div><div class="rw-title">Vulnerable and underserved populations are disproportionately affected when AI-powered technology and cybersecurity are not designed with everyone in mind</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">When AI-powered technology is built without accounting for the full range of people who will encounter it, the gaps tend to fall hardest on those least equipped to navigate around them: elderly users, people with disabilities, children and teenagers, low-literacy users, those with mental health conditions, and others whose needs fall outside the assumptions of most technology design. Research across HCI, accessibility, and cybersecurity has documented how design decisions made without representative input produce systems that work well for some users and poorly for others. What remains less understood is how these dynamics play out in AI-powered contexts, and across intersecting vulnerabilities. This dissertation draws on existing scholarship to examine where those gaps are most consequential, and what organizations, policymakers, and technology professionals can do to build and implement more equitably.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">5</div><div class="rw-title">Trust in technology and AI is eroding</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">Across every demographic, trust in AI and technology systems is declining. People do not know when they are interacting with AI, do not understand what it is doing with their data, and do not believe it is working in their interest. That erosion of trust has real consequences - people avoid healthcare technology, disengage from financial systems, and make themselves more vulnerable by trying to opt out of systems they cannot trust. This research examines what builds and breaks that trust and how design can restore it.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">6</div><div class="rw-title">Legal compliance is failing real people</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">Laws like the ADA, GDPR, CCPA, COPPA, and emerging AI regulations exist to protect people. A recurring pattern in the existing literature is that legal compliance is treated as a checkbox rather than a genuine design commitment - producing systems that are technically compliant but practically inaccessible or unsafe for real users. This research draws on what is already known about the relationship between UX design, accessibility, and legal frameworks to examine how that gap manifests in AI-powered technology and cybersecurity contexts, and what applied guidance can help organizations move from performative compliance toward protection that actually works.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">7</div><div class="rw-title">Cybersecurity is a human problem, not just a technical one</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">Research consistently shows that a significant proportion of security incidents involve a human element - someone clicking a phishing link, ignoring a warning, or failing to respond to an alert in time. The question of how human behavior, cognitive load, interface design, and psychological factors shape security outcomes is not new to the literature. What remains less examined is how AI is changing that equation - making threats more sophisticated, making security interfaces more complex, and placing greater cognitive demands on both everyday users and security professionals. This research applies existing knowledge about human factors in cybersecurity to the AI-powered context, where the human dimension is more consequential than ever.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">8</div><div class="rw-title">Mental health and technology intersect in ways nobody is designing for</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">People experiencing depression, anxiety, PTSD, ADHD, and other conditions interact with technology in ways that may differ meaningfully from users without those conditions - and existing research suggests this has implications for how they engage with AI-powered tools and cybersecurity systems. This dissertation draws on what is already known about mental health, cognition, and human-computer interaction to examine how those patterns intersect with AI and cybersecurity contexts, with the goal of helping organizations, technology professionals, and policymakers better understand and account for this population in the systems they build and deploy.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">9</div><div class="rw-title">Businesses and governments are building and deploying AI without a humanistic framework</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">Businesses and governments are both building and deploying AI-powered technology and cybersecurity systems - and in either role, many are doing so without a sufficient understanding of how the humanistic approach should inform those decisions. An organization building its own AI-powered product carries responsibility for what it creates and how it behaves. An organization deploying a third-party AI tool carries responsibility for how it is implemented, configured, and used within their context. In both cases, the human-centered obligations do not disappear because the technology was made elsewhere or implemented by someone else. Organizations across both roles need guidance on how to account for human behavior, protect vulnerable populations, meet their legal and ethical obligations, and build implementation practices that go beyond technical requirements alone.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">10</div><div class="rw-title">People with disabilities are doubly excluded - by design failures and by cybercrime</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">People with disabilities face a two-front problem. First, AI and cybersecurity systems are often designed without them in mind, creating access barriers, missed security warnings, and interfaces that assistive technologies cannot navigate. Second, those same accessibility needs can be exploited by bad actors. A person using voice control may expose credentials in shared spaces. A person with low vision cannot verify URLs visually. A person with cognitive decline may be unable to detect a phishing attempt designed to look legitimate. Disability is not a single category but a spectrum of intersecting conditions, each carrying distinct implications for how people interact with technology and how they can be harmed through it. Most existing cybersecurity guidance was not written with any of them in mind.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="real-world-card">
      <div class="rw-hdr"><div class="rw-num">11</div><div class="rw-title">AI is making existing cybersecurity threats against vulnerable populations more effective and harder to counter</div></div>
      <div class="rw-body">Cybersecurity threats targeting vulnerable populations are not new. Romance scams, grandparent fraud, and financial impersonation have existed for years. What AI has changed is the scale, sophistication, and psychological precision of those attacks, and how inadequate traditional protective guidance has become. An AI-powered romance scam maintains consistent personas over months, adapts to its target's emotional responses, and generates voice, images, and video to reinforce the deception. For someone experiencing cognitive decline, research on social cognition suggests the interaction may be deeply convincing in ways that are difficult to resist. The standard guidance loses traction when the person has formed a months-long emotional bond with what they believe is a real relationship. This research examines not only how to design better systems, but what meaningful protective guidance looks like for people whose cognitive and psychological state makes standard guidance difficult or impossible to act on.</div>
    </div>

  </div>

