    <!-- Tab: What Is AI -->
    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-what">
      <div class="sentence-block">
        <div class="sentence-label">Working Definition</div>
        <div class="sentence-text">"Artificial intelligence refers to computer systems designed to perform tasks that typically require human intelligence - such as understanding language, recognizing patterns, making decisions, and generating content - by learning from data rather than following rigid, hand-written rules."</div>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="bullet-card">
          <div class="bullet-card-hdr">Core AI Types</div>
          <div class="bullet-list">
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Narrow AI</strong> - Designed for one specific task. Most AI in use today: spam filters, recommendation engines, fraud detection.</div>
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Generative AI</strong> - Creates new content (text, images, code, audio) by learning patterns. ChatGPT, Claude, DALL-E, and Gemini fall here.</div>
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Machine Learning</strong> - Systems that improve through exposure to data, rather than being explicitly programmed with rules.</div>
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Large Language Models (LLMs)</strong> - AI trained on vast text datasets to understand and generate human language at scale.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bullet-card">
          <div class="bullet-card-hdr">Key Characteristics</div>
          <div class="bullet-list">
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Pattern recognition</strong> - Identifies structure in data at a scale and speed no human could match.</div>
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Probabilistic outputs</strong> - Produces answers based on likelihood, not certainty. This is why AI can hallucinate or mislead.</div>
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Opacity</strong> - Many AI systems cannot explain their own reasoning, creating the "black box" problem.</div>
            <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Training data dependency</strong> - AI reflects the biases, gaps, and assumptions in the data it was trained on.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sentence-block">
        <div class="sentence-label">Why This Matters for the Dissertation</div>
        <div class="sentence-text">"AI is not a single technology with a single effect on people - it is a diverse set of systems that interact with humans in very different ways depending on context, design, and deployment. Cybersecurity is increasingly inseparable from that picture: AI is reshaping how threats are generated, how defenses are built, and how security decisions are made. And human-computer interaction is the lens through which both must be understood - because at the center of every AI system and every cybersecurity decision is a human being whose experience, comprehension, trust, and safety determine whether those systems succeed or fail. Understanding what AI actually is and how it intersects with cybersecurity and HCI is foundational to the applied research this dissertation pursues."</div>
      </div>
    </div>

