    <!-- Tab: How People Feel About AI -->
    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-feel">
      <div class="sentence-block">
        <div class="sentence-label">The Public Sentiment Landscape</div>
        <div class="sentence-text">"Public attitudes toward AI are not uniform - they vary dramatically by age, education, sector of use, prior experience, and whether someone has had a positive or negative encounter with an AI system. What research consistently shows is that most people hold AI at arm's length: aware of it, uncertain about it, and often unable to clearly articulate either their concerns or their trust."</div>
      </div>
      <div class="sentiment-grid">
        <div class="sentiment-card">
          <div class="sentiment-hdr pos">Where People Are Optimistic</div>
          <div class="sentiment-list">
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div>Medical AI for earlier disease detection and diagnosis is broadly welcomed, especially by people who have experienced delayed diagnosis.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div>AI tools that save time on repetitive tasks are viewed positively, particularly by workers who feel AI handles the burden and not the judgment.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div>Young people and tech-comfortable users tend to show more openness, viewing AI as a useful assistant rather than a threat.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div>Accessibility features powered by AI - such as captioning, screen readers, and translation - receive strong positive reception from disability communities.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div>AI in low-stakes, personal productivity contexts (scheduling, recommendations, organization) tends to generate positive responses with low anxiety.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sentiment-card">
          <div class="sentiment-hdr neg">Where People Are Anxious or Distrustful</div>
          <div class="sentiment-list">
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div>Job displacement is one of the most consistent sources of fear - a majority of workers in multiple surveys express concern that AI will reduce employment in their field.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div>Surveillance and privacy concerns are widespread, particularly around facial recognition, behavioral tracking, and data collection without clear consent.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div>AI in high-stakes decisions - criminal justice, hiring, benefits - generates the strongest distrust, particularly among communities that have experienced algorithmic discrimination.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div>Many people report not knowing when they are interacting with AI at all, and feeling deceived or unsettled when they discover they were.</div>
            <div class="sentiment-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div>Parents express particular anxiety about AI's impact on children - around social media algorithms, AI companions, and content generation targeting minors.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bullet-card">
        <div class="bullet-card-hdr">Demographic Patterns in AI Sentiment</div>
        <div class="bullet-list">
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Age</strong> - Older adults tend to express more caution and less familiarity. Teenagers and young adults are more likely to use AI tools regularly but may underestimate risks.</div>
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Education and income</strong> - Higher education and income are associated with both greater AI use and greater critical awareness of its limitations - but not always greater safety.</div>
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Race and ethnicity</strong> - Communities historically subject to surveillance and algorithmic discrimination show measurably lower trust in AI systems, particularly those deployed by law enforcement or government.</div>
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Sector of exposure</strong> - People who have experienced AI negatively (a denied loan, a wrong diagnosis, a moderation error) carry lasting distrust that generalizes well beyond the original system.</div>
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div><strong>Children and teens</strong> - Research on how young people feel about AI is still emerging, but early evidence suggests teens have high engagement with AI tools alongside low understanding of how those tools work, what data they collect, and who controls them.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Tab: Pros & Cons of Human-AI Interaction -->
    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-proscon">
      <div class="sentence-block">
        <div class="sentence-label">The Dual Nature of Human-AI Interaction</div>
        <div class="sentence-text">"AI interaction is not inherently beneficial or harmful - its impact depends almost entirely on how a system is designed, who it is designed for, how transparently it operates, and whether the humans interacting with it have the literacy, access, and power to understand and influence what it does."</div>
      </div>
      <div class="proscon-grid">
        <div class="proscon-card pros">
          <div class="proscon-hdr">Potential Benefits</div>
          <div class="proscon-list">
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div><strong>Accessibility gains</strong> - AI can reduce barriers for users with disabilities, language differences, or low literacy when designed with those populations in mind.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div><strong>Decision support</strong> - AI can surface relevant information at the right moment, helping users make better-informed decisions without requiring expert knowledge.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div><strong>Speed and availability</strong> - AI systems operate 24/7 and respond instantly, expanding access to services that previously required waiting, scheduling, or geographic proximity.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div><strong>Error reduction in routine tasks</strong> - In well-defined, low-ambiguity contexts, AI consistently outperforms humans on precision and reduces costly mistakes.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div><strong>Personalization</strong> - AI can tailor experiences, content, and security responses to individual user behavior, improving relevance and reducing friction.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-pos"></div><strong>Scale</strong> - AI makes it possible to extend services to far larger populations than human workforces alone could serve, including in low-resource or underserved communities.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="proscon-card cons">
          <div class="proscon-hdr">Known Risks and Harms</div>
          <div class="proscon-list">
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div><strong>Opacity and loss of accountability</strong> - When AI makes consequential decisions that humans cannot understand or contest, accountability disappears and harm goes unaddressed.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div><strong>Automation bias</strong> - People over-trust AI recommendations, deferring to algorithmic outputs even when they are wrong, especially in high-stakes or high-pressure situations.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div><strong>Bias and discrimination</strong> - AI systems trained on historical data reproduce and amplify historical inequities, harming already-marginalized populations disproportionately.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div><strong>Cognitive and emotional dependency</strong> - Prolonged interaction with AI systems can erode independent judgment, critical thinking, and human relationship skills - particularly in younger users still developing these capacities.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div><strong>Security exploitation</strong> - Bad actors use AI to generate more convincing phishing, deepfakes, and social engineering at scale, making every human user a more vulnerable target.</div>
            <div class="proscon-item"><div class="s-dot-neg"></div><strong>Privacy erosion</strong> - Human-AI interaction generates enormous amounts of behavioral data that can be exploited, sold, or used to surveil without users' meaningful understanding or consent.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bullet-card">
        <div class="bullet-card-hdr">How This Directly Informs the Research</div>
        <div class="bullet-list">
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>The benefits of AI interaction are real - but they are not evenly distributed. They accrue most to users who already have digital literacy, technological access, and system familiarity.</div>
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>The harms of AI interaction are also real - and they concentrate in vulnerable populations who have the least capacity to recognize, resist, or recover from them.</div>
          <div class="bullet-item"><div class="bullet-dot"></div>Human-centered design is the mechanism through which the benefits can be expanded and the harms can be reduced. This dissertation builds the research foundation that makes that possible.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

