    <div class="sec-hdr"><span class="sec-title">Dissertation Chapter Drafts, Research Proposal</span></div>
    <div id="chapters-wrapper">
    <div class="sentence-block">
      <div class="sentence-label">Current Status</div>
      <div class="sentence-text">"This is a research proposal map, not an active dissertation. It outlines the topics, questions, and chapter structure being developed as part of the application and planning process for the Purdue Doctor of Technology program. The D.Tech. is distinguished from a PhD in that it applies and synthesizes existing knowledge to address a real-world industry gap - rather than generating new theory. The chapters below reflect proposed research directions and will evolve through committee formation, feedback, and the formal research design process."</div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 1 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">1</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Introduction: The Human Side of AI and Cybersecurity</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Problem Statement · Significance · Research Questions · Overview</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter establishes why human-centered research in AI and cybersecurity is both urgent and underserved. It introduces the central tension of the dissertation: AI and cybersecurity are increasingly consequential for everyday people, yet the systems that govern these technologies are still largely designed by and for technically sophisticated users. It names the problem clearly, articulates why it matters, and lays out the research questions that will structure the work.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Problem statement:</strong> AI-powered technology and cybersecurity systems are being deployed across every sector, but the human experience of interacting with these systems - trust, usability, accessibility, comprehension - is rarely centered in their design.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Significance:</strong> The populations most dependent on these systems are often the least equipped to navigate them safely. Vulnerable populations - including elderly users, those with disabilities, low-literacy users, people with mental health conditions, and children and teenagers - face disproportionate harm when these systems fail or exclude them.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Research questions:</strong> How do people form and lose trust in AI? How does UX design shape security behavior and legal compliance? What happens when AI systems are not designed with vulnerable populations in mind? How do children and teens navigate AI-mediated environments without the tools to understand them?</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Overview of approach:</strong> Applied, interdisciplinary research drawing on human-centered computing, cybersecurity, psychology, and legal/policy frameworks. Grounded in real-world contexts and practitioner-oriented in its outputs.</div>
        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, topic outline in development</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 2 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">2</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Literature Review: Trust, AI, and the Human Experience of Technology</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Theoretical Framework · Prior Research · Identified Gaps</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter surveys existing scholarship across the intersecting fields the dissertation draws on: human-computer interaction, trust in automation, cybersecurity UX, accessibility, AI ethics, and emerging research on vulnerable populations in digital environments. It identifies where the literature converges, where it conflicts, and most importantly - where the gaps are that this dissertation will address.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Trust in AI systems:</strong> What existing research tells us about how trust is formed, calibrated, broken, and restored in human-AI relationships - and the specific factors (transparency, explainability, perceived competence, alignment) that drive it.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Cybersecurity as a human problem:</strong> Literature on human error in security, social engineering, security fatigue, and the behavioral psychology of threat response.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>UX design and compliance:</strong> Research on how interface design shapes legally significant user behavior, including consent, disclosure, and data privacy decisions.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Vulnerable populations in AI and technology:</strong> What we know - and what is largely missing - about how elderly users, people with disabilities, low-literacy users, mental health populations, and children and teens experience AI-mediated systems. Particular attention to the disability literature and the intersecting vulnerabilities compounding effect.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI as an amplifier of existing cybersecurity threats:</strong> Emerging literature on how generative AI has transformed previously well-understood threat categories - romance scams, phishing, impersonation fraud, sextortion - and what that demands of researchers, designers, and policymakers. Identifies the gap between existing protective guidance and current reality, particularly for vulnerable populations.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI literacy and risk perception:</strong> Research on how people understand (or misunderstand) what AI is and does, and how that shapes their risk behavior online and offline.</div>
        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, pending formal research design</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 3 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">3</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Generative AI and the Threat Landscape: Risks, Harms, and Human Vulnerability</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Deepfakes · NCII · Sextortion · Chatbots · Generative Audio · Children, Women, and Cross-Population Harms</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter examines generative AI not as a neutral tool but as a category of technology that produces specific, documented harms against real people - with particular focus on how those harms concentrate in women, children and teenagers, and other vulnerable populations. It covers how AI-generated imagery, video, audio, and conversational agents are being weaponized for non-consensual sexual exploitation, sextortion, grooming, fraud, and psychological harm. It also examines the structural conditions - legal gaps, platform failures, technological accessibility - that allow these harms to scale.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Deepfakes and synthetic video:</strong> How AI-generated video and image manipulation works and the documented categories of harm it has produced: impersonation, fabricated evidence, political disinformation, financial fraud, and the weaponization of synthetic media against private individuals.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Non-consensual intimate imagery (NCII) and AI-generated sexual content:</strong> Women are disproportionately targeted. This section examines psychological, professional, and social harms to victims; the challenge of getting content removed; and the gap between legal frameworks and the speed of the technology.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI-facilitated sextortion - a cross-population threat:</strong> AI tools can generate convincing synthetic sexual imagery from ordinary photos, lowering the barrier to extortion dramatically. Men and boys are increasingly represented among victims. This section examines why the synthetic nature of imagery does not reduce the harm - legally, emotionally, or socially.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Adolescents and AI-enabled exploitation:</strong> Scammers building fake personas, fabricating imagery from public photos, and using it as extortion leverage even when no real images were ever shared. Includes the documented psychological impact - including cases of suicide - and the argument that AI-generated imagery carries the same harm weight as real imagery for an adolescent on the receiving end of a threat.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Chatbots and conversational AI as vectors of harm:</strong> Romance fraud, social engineering, AI-powered grooming, and the specific risk for elderly users who may form genuine emotional attachments to chatbot personas they believe to be human.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Generative audio and voice cloning:</strong> AI impersonation of trusted individuals - family members, employers - in fraud schemes. The psychological dimension of why voice is so trusted and why its synthetic replication is so dangerous.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI-assisted phishing at scale:</strong> The collapse of traditional detection heuristics (spelling errors, suspicious grammar) now that LLMs can generate highly personalized, convincing phishing content at volume.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Disability, cognitive decline, and AI-enhanced exploitation:</strong> How specific disability categories intersect with the generative AI threat landscape. People experiencing cognitive decline are precisely the target that AI-enhanced romance scams and impersonation fraud are optimized to exploit - and standard protective guidance loses traction when the person cannot reliably detect the threat.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Trust collapse and epistemic harm:</strong> When any image, video, or audio can plausibly be fabricated, the evidentiary and social value of documentation is undermined. Victims asserting imagery is fabricated may not be believed. This section examines what that means for justice and accountability.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Legal and regulatory landscape:</strong> State-level NCII legislation, STOP CSAM Act provisions, COPPA protections, and where significant gaps persist. The difficulty of holding AI tool providers liable for enabled harms.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Design, platform, and policy implications:</strong> AI watermarking, platform takedown obligations, age verification, detection tool accessibility, and media literacy as a public health and child safety imperative.</div>
        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, topic outline in development</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 4 -->

    <!-- CH 4 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">4</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Ethics, Policy, and Law at the Intersection of HCI, AI, and Cybersecurity</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Legal Frameworks · Ethical Dimensions · Policy Landscape · Where the Three Disciplines Converge</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter examines the legal, ethical, and policy frameworks that govern the intersection of human-computer interaction, artificial intelligence, and cybersecurity. It does not treat these as three separate bodies of law operating in isolation, but as an interconnected regulatory landscape that organizations, technologists, and policymakers must navigate together. Just as HCI, AI, and cybersecurity cannot be separated as disciplines, the legal and ethical obligations surrounding them cannot be fully understood in isolation either. This chapter is not written as a legal treatise - it is written for technology professionals, organizations, and practitioners who need to understand the legal and ethical environment they operate in, and why that understanding is inseparable from human-centered design, AI, and cybersecurity practice. The chapter also examines where current law falls short of the protection it promises, where ethics must fill the gaps that legislation has not yet closed, and what the rapidly evolving policy environment means for organizations operating in a landscape where the rules are still being written.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The ethics of human-centered design:</strong> Design is never neutral. Every interface decision, every default setting, every piece of consent language reflects a set of values and assumptions about who the user is and what they deserve to understand. This section examines the ethical obligations of HCI practitioners: designing for accessibility and inclusion, avoiding dark patterns and manipulative design, ensuring meaningful consent rather than performative compliance, and accounting for the full range of users rather than the assumed average. It also examines the intersection of design ethics with power, asking who gets to decide what constitutes good design and whose experience is centered in that judgment.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>HCI and the law, accessibility, privacy, and consent:</strong> The legal framework governing human-computer interaction includes accessibility law (ADA, Section 508, EU Web Accessibility Directive), privacy and data protection regulation (GDPR, CCPA, COPPA), and consumer protection requirements around disclosure and consent. This section examines how these frameworks shape what organizations must build, where they consistently fall short of genuine protection, and the gap between legal compliance and human-centered design. It also examines GDPR's right to explanation for automated decisions as a legal recognition that interface design has ethical stakes, and what that means for AI-powered systems specifically.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI ethics, the emerging regulatory landscape, and what organizations must know:</strong> The ethics of artificial intelligence has moved from academic discourse to regulatory mandate at unprecedented speed. This section examines the emerging AI policy landscape: the EU AI Act and its risk-tiered approach to AI regulation, US executive orders and agency guidance on AI governance, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, and sector-specific AI rules emerging in healthcare, financial services, and employment. It also examines the foundational ethical principles that run through these frameworks, including fairness, accountability, transparency, and explainability, and asks what those principles require of organizations that are building or deploying AI-powered technology in practice rather than in theory.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI bias, discrimination, and legal liability:</strong> When AI systems produce biased outputs that disadvantage protected classes in hiring, lending, healthcare, or housing, they may create legal liability under existing civil rights and anti-discrimination law even before AI-specific regulation applies. This section examines the legal and ethical dimensions of algorithmic bias: the documented cases where AI systems have produced discriminatory outcomes, the legal frameworks that may apply, the challenge of proving discrimination when the decision-making process is opaque, and what organizations must do to assess and address bias in the AI systems they build and deploy.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Cybersecurity law, policy, and the human dimension:</strong> The cybersecurity legal landscape is broad and fragmented: HIPAA governs healthcare data, GLBA governs financial institutions, GDPR and CCPA govern consumer data broadly, the SEC has issued cybersecurity disclosure rules for public companies, and critical infrastructure is governed by sector-specific frameworks. NIST's Cybersecurity Framework provides voluntary guidance that has become a de facto standard. This section examines how these frameworks account for, or fail to account for, the human dimension of cybersecurity: the interface design, user behavior, and organizational decision-making that determine whether technical security controls actually protect real people.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The ethics of cybersecurity in an AI-powered threat landscape:</strong> AI has changed the cybersecurity ethics landscape in two ways simultaneously. On the offense side, AI enables attacks of greater sophistication, scale, and psychological precision than were previously possible, raising ethical questions about the responsibility of AI tool developers for enabled harms. On the defense side, AI-powered security systems make autonomous decisions that affect people, including decisions that may lock users out of accounts, flag transactions, or generate alerts that shape security outcomes without meaningful human review. This section examines the ethical obligations of organizations and security professionals operating in this landscape, including the duty of transparency about AI-driven decisions and the obligation to design security systems that do not disproportionately harm vulnerable users.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Where HCI, AI, and cybersecurity law intersect and where they conflict:</strong> The three legal landscapes do not always align. Accessibility requirements may create usability features that introduce security vulnerabilities. AI transparency requirements may conflict with proprietary model protections. Security controls mandated by one framework may create data processing activities governed by another. This section maps the points of intersection and tension, and examines how organizations can navigate a regulatory environment where the obligations of HCI, AI, and cybersecurity do not always point in the same direction.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The gap between compliance and genuine protection:</strong> Legal compliance is a floor, not a ceiling. A system can be technically compliant with accessibility law and still be functionally inaccessible to real users. A system can satisfy GDPR consent requirements and still fail to give users meaningful understanding of what they are agreeing to. A cybersecurity program can check every required box and still leave vulnerable populations exposed. This section examines why the gap between legal compliance and genuine human protection persists, and what it demands of organizations that treat compliance as a minimum obligation rather than a design goal.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Ethics when law has not caught up:</strong> The pace of AI development has outrun the pace of legislation. There are domains where organizations face genuine ethical obligations that no current law enforces, including the use of AI in contexts where vulnerable populations are at particular risk, the deployment of AI systems whose failure modes are not yet well understood, and the collection of behavioral data at scales that existing privacy frameworks were not designed to govern. This section examines what ethical responsibility looks like in a regulatory gap, drawing on existing ethics frameworks and the research literature to identify what responsible practice requires in the absence of clear legal mandate.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The evolving policy landscape and its implications for this research:</strong> The policy environment surrounding HCI, AI, and cybersecurity is changing faster than at any prior point in the history of technology regulation. This section surveys the active legislative and regulatory landscape, noting where frameworks are being developed, where they are being contested, and what the trajectory of change means for organizations trying to build responsibly for the long term. It also returns to the dissertation's central argument: that organizations navigating this landscape need an integrated understanding of HCI, AI, and cybersecurity ethics and law, not three separate legal checklists, because the problems they face and the obligations they carry do not respect those divisions.</div>

        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, topic outline in development</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 5 -->

    <!-- CH 5 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">5</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Disability, AI, and Cybersecurity: Access, Exploitation, and the Path Toward Inclusive Security</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Accessibility as Attack Surface · Inaccessible Security Systems · AI as Tool and Threat · Designing for Inclusion</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">People with disabilities sit at a uniquely acute intersection of HCI, AI, and cybersecurity. They depend on assistive technology to interact with the digital world, yet that same technology can introduce security vulnerabilities. They face cybersecurity systems designed without them in mind, yet the consequences of those exclusions are not merely inconvenient - they are materially dangerous. And AI, which holds genuine promise as an accessibility tool, simultaneously introduces new forms of harm and exclusion for this population. This chapter examines all three dimensions: how the assistive and adaptive technologies that people with disabilities rely on can become vectors for exploitation; how cybersecurity systems fail people with disabilities through inaccessible design; and how AI is both expanding access and amplifying risk for this population. Rather than treating disability as a static category, this chapter examines it as a spectrum of intersecting differences, each carrying distinct implications, and draws on existing research to ask what genuinely inclusive, secure, and usable technology design requires.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Accessibility tools as attack surface:</strong> The assistive technologies that enable people with disabilities to use digital systems can themselves become vectors for exploitation. Screen readers that announce content aloud may expose sensitive information in shared environments. Voice control interfaces that accept spoken commands can be hijacked by audio attacks or expose credentials when used in public. Eye-tracking and switch-access devices that monitor physical movement can reveal patterns of behavior that compromise privacy. Predictive and augmentative communication tools that store vocabulary and frequently used phrases may hold sensitive personal information. This section examines the specific ways that accessibility accommodations, precisely because they modify the standard human-technology interface, can open pathways for attack that standard cybersecurity guidance was never designed to address. The people who depend most on these tools are often the least able to identify or recover from the exploitation they enable.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Inaccessible cybersecurity systems and the harm of exclusion:</strong> Cybersecurity mechanisms are among the least accessible components of digital systems. CAPTCHA challenges rely on visual or audio perception that may be unavailable to users with sensory disabilities. Multi-factor authentication flows assume physical dexterity, functional vision, and cognitive capacity to process time-limited codes under pressure. Security warnings are written in technical language that presupposes literacy and comprehension levels that many users, including those with cognitive disabilities, do not have. Password complexity requirements generate cognitive load that disproportionately burdens users with memory impairments. When cybersecurity systems are inaccessible, the harm is not merely that disabled users are inconvenienced. The harm is that they face a forced choice: accept workarounds that reduce security, rely on assistance that compromises privacy, or disengage from systems they need. This section examines documented patterns of inaccessible security design, the populations most affected, and the research on what genuinely accessible security design requires as distinct from merely compliant design.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI as an accessibility tool: genuine gains and their limits:</strong> Artificial intelligence has produced some of the most significant accessibility advances in decades. AI-powered captioning and transcription have expanded communication access for users who are deaf or hard of hearing. Image description tools have given blind and low-vision users meaningful access to visual content. Predictive text and AI writing assistants have reduced the cognitive and motor burden of text-based communication. Voice-to-text and text-to-voice conversion have enabled fuller participation in digital environments for users with motor and communication disabilities. These are real gains and this section examines them honestly. It also examines their limits: AI accessibility tools that fail in noisy environments, that generate inaccurate descriptions of complex or culturally specific imagery, that are not built into security-critical interfaces where they would matter most, and that serve as accessibility layers over fundamentally inaccessible systems rather than genuinely inclusive design from the ground up.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI as harm amplifier for people with disabilities:</strong> The same AI capabilities that open access for some users with disabilities introduce new forms of harm and exclusion for others. Facial recognition systems have documented accuracy disparities for people with facial differences, dark skin tones, and atypical facial musculature. Voice authentication systems exclude users with speech disabilities, progressive neurological conditions, or atypical vocal patterns. Automated content moderation trained on neurotypical communication norms may disproportionately flag or remove communication from users with autism spectrum conditions, cognitive disabilities, or AAC device users whose output differs in structure from expected patterns. AI systems used in hiring, benefits determination, and healthcare risk scoring have shown bias patterns that compound existing disadvantage for people with disabilities. This section examines how AI systems, when built without representative training data and without human-centered evaluation of their outcomes across disability categories, can systematically exclude and harm the populations they were never designed to serve.</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The compounding intersection: cognitive decline, AI-enhanced threats, and the limits of protective guidance:</strong> For people experiencing progressive cognitive conditions, including Alzheimer's disease, other dementias, and acquired brain injury, the intersection of disability, AI, and cybersecurity produces a particularly acute and underexamined risk profile. These individuals may have well-established digital habits and social networks formed before cognitive decline, making them active users of platforms and services that now present risks they are less equipped to detect. AI-enhanced social engineering, voice cloning, and chatbot-based fraud are optimized for precisely the psychological and cognitive vulnerabilities that cognitive decline produces: trust in familiar voices, difficulty detecting inconsistency over time, susceptibility to emotional manipulation. This section examines what the existing research tells us about this intersection and asks a harder question that the literature has not fully addressed: what does protective guidance look like for someone whose capacity to act on standard advice is itself compromised, and what obligations does that place on the organizations whose systems they are using?</div>

          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Toward genuinely inclusive security design:</strong> This section draws on the chapter's analysis to examine what genuinely inclusive, secure, and usable design requires as distinct from accessible design as a compliance target. It covers: principles for building security mechanisms that do not force disabled users to choose between access and protection; the role of co-design with disability communities in security product development; standards and frameworks for accessibility in security-critical interfaces; AI audit and evaluation requirements that include disability representation; and the organizational accountability structures that ensure accessibility and security are not treated as competing priorities. The argument this section makes is consistent with the dissertation's broader framing: designing security and AI systems for the full range of human difference does not produce a weaker or more compromised product. It produces a more robust one, because the design processes that surface disability-related failure modes also surface failure modes that affect everyone.</div>

        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, topic outline in development</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">6</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Methodology: Research Design for Human-Centered AI Study</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Research Approach · Data Collection · Analysis Framework</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter outlines how the research will be conducted. The methodology draws from multiple traditions - qualitative methods (interviews, usability studies, focus groups), quantitative measurement (surveys, behavioral data), and design-based approaches (framework development, prototype evaluation). The methodology must be grounded in human-centered research principles and appropriate for the vulnerable populations involved.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Research paradigm:</strong> Applied, mixed-methods, interdisciplinary - drawing from HCI, behavioral psychology, and cybersecurity research traditions.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Participant populations:</strong> Identifying and ethically recruiting participants from vulnerable population groups, with particular attention to informed consent processes for minors, elderly participants, and individuals with disabilities.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Data collection methods:</strong> Structured interviews, usability observation, survey instruments measuring trust and risk perception, and analysis of existing AI system interactions.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Ethical considerations:</strong> IRB requirements, data privacy, power dynamics in research with vulnerable populations, and responsible AI use in the research process itself.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Expected outputs:</strong> Applied frameworks, design guidelines, or policy recommendations that practitioners can implement - consistent with the D.Tech.'s professional doctorate emphasis.</div>
        </div>
