    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">7</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Findings: How People Actually Experience AI and Cybersecurity Systems</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Research Results · Patterns · Population-Specific Observations</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter presents what the research found - the patterns, behaviors, and experiences documented across the study populations. It is organized around the core research questions and includes population-specific findings, with particular attention to how experiences differ across vulnerable groups including children and teenagers, elderly users, and those with disabilities or mental health conditions.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Trust formation and breakdown:</strong> How participants formed, maintained, and lost trust in AI systems - and what design factors drove those shifts.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Vulnerable population experiences:</strong> What was distinctly different about how children and teens, elderly users, users with disabilities, and others in the focus populations experienced the same systems.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Security behavior and UX:</strong> What the data shows about how interface design shapes whether people make safe or unsafe decisions - and how AI changes that dynamic.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>AI comprehension and misconceptions:</strong> What participants understood - and misunderstood - about how AI worked, what it was doing, and who was responsible for its outputs.</div>
