    <!-- CH 6 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">8</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Implementing AI and Cybersecurity in the Real World: A Human-Centered Framework for Business, Government, and NGOs</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Organizational Implementation · HCI as Operational Lens · Legal &amp; Ethical Frameworks · Risk Across Sectors · Applied D.Tech. Contribution</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter is the applied core of the dissertation - the place where the research meets practice. Where earlier chapters establish what is known and what the research found, this chapter addresses the real-world industry gap the dissertation exists to close: organizations across sectors are building and deploying AI-powered technology and cybersecurity systems without a sufficient human-centered framework to guide those decisions. Human-Computer Interaction is the operating lens throughout - and the output is practical guidance grounded in existing knowledge, not a new theory invented from scratch.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Where organizational implementation breaks down:</strong> The standard implementation lifecycle - procurement, deployment, training, monitoring - and where human-centered considerations are systematically absent: requirements gathering that excludes end users, accessibility reviews that happen after design, security UX as afterthought, and vulnerable population needs never assessed at all.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The private sector:</strong> FinTech, healthcare, insurance, retail, legal services. Competitive pressures driving speed-over-safety decisions. Compliance treated as a ceiling rather than a floor. The specific challenge of customer-facing AI products whose user populations include vulnerable groups the organization has never studied.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Government agencies and public sector:</strong> Federal, state, and local agencies serving constituencies with high concentrations of vulnerable populations. Procurement barriers to human-centered design. Section 508, ADA Title II, emerging AI governance frameworks - and where they fall short.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Non-governmental organizations:</strong> Mission-driven tech implementation under resource constraints. How NGOs adopt donated or discounted tools and what risks that creates for the people they directly serve. Ethical obligations when deploying AI in humanitarian response, crisis counseling, and social services.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Legal and ethical frameworks:</strong> ADA, CCPA, COPPA, HIPAA, GDPR, EU AI Act, FTC guidelines. The gap between what current law requires and what genuine human-centered protection demands. Why compliance is necessary but deeply insufficient.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Risk management with a human-centered lens:</strong> Integrating human-centered risk assessment into NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, and NIST CSF - identifying not just technical vulnerabilities but human ones: populations who cannot safely use a given system, threat vectors that exploit psychology rather than software, organizational decisions that create human risk even when technical security is strong.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Toward a human-centered AI implementation framework:</strong> Drawing on existing bodies of knowledge across HCI, cybersecurity, accessibility, and organizational management, this section synthesizes what the applied research reveals into practical guidance organizations can use. Rather than proposing an entirely new theory, the goal is to bring together what is already known in a way that is actionable for the specific challenge of human-centered AI and cybersecurity implementation - organized around the implementation lifecycle and applicable across sectors.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>HCI as an organizational implementation discipline:</strong> What it looks like to treat HCI operationally rather than academically - embedded in requirements gathering, interface design, usability testing with actual populations, and post-deployment monitoring that tracks human outcomes alongside technical performance. Who carries HCI responsibility, and what happens when no one does.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>HCI inside the cybersecurity sector - the full professional workforce:</strong> The cybersecurity professional as a user whose own tools are often poorly designed. Covers the full workforce - analysts, penetration testers, threat hunters, incident responders, GRC professionals, security engineers, DevSecOps practitioners, CISOs. Alert fatigue, cognitive load, role-specific tool design, visualization and decision support, proactive vs. reactive design, workforce burnout. Organizational tool-building obligations and the integration/interoperability layer - context lost at the handoff between tools is its own security failure mode. Good design in professional security tools is itself a security control.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Technology and cybersecurity professionals as AI adopters and builders:</strong> Cybersecurity professionals exist across every sector - not only technology. All technology professionals (developers, data scientists, cloud architects, product managers, IT professionals, consultants) are actively adopting and building AI. The risks: over-reliance on AI outputs under time pressure, data exposure through external AI tools, hallucinations entering reports and risk assessments, and the adversarial mirror problem - the same AI capabilities improving their efficiency are improving adversaries' capabilities simultaneously. Cybersecurity teams are themselves organizations implementing AI and are subject to the same human-centered imperative they advocate for clients. The implementation failures of technology professionals and cybersecurity professionals are connected and propagate across each other. The framework applies to everyone in the chain - including the professionals whose job it is to secure everyone else.</div>
        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, topic outline in development</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 7 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">9</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Discussion: Implications for Design, Policy, and Practice</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">What It Means · Recommendations · Limitations · Future Directions</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter interprets what the findings mean for the people and organizations responsible for building, deploying, and regulating AI systems. It bridges research and practice - translating empirical insights into actionable frameworks for designers, policymakers, practitioners, and organizations working with vulnerable populations. It also honestly addresses the study's limitations and identifies directions for future research.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Design implications:</strong> What the findings mean for how AI-powered technology and cybersecurity systems should be designed differently - particularly for and with vulnerable populations including children and teens.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Policy implications:</strong> What the findings suggest about gaps in existing regulation (ADA, GDPR, CCPA, COPPA, emerging AI legislation) and where new frameworks are needed.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Organizational practice:</strong> Guidance for businesses and government entities on how to implement AI-powered systems in ways that are equitable, transparent, and protective of vulnerable users.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Limitations and future research:</strong> Honest accounting of what this study could not address, what would need to be studied next, and where the field most urgently needs additional scholarship.</div>
        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, content pending research findings</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CH 8 -->
    <div class="chapter-card">
      <div class="chapter-hdr">
        <div class="chapter-num">10</div>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
          <div class="chapter-title">Conclusion: Toward Human-Centered AI and Cybersecurity</div>
          <div class="chapter-sub">Contribution to Knowledge · Closing Argument · Call to Action</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chapter-body">
        <div class="chapter-purpose">This chapter closes the dissertation by clearly stating what it has applied, synthesized, and added to practice - and why that matters for the organizations, professionals, and populations at the center of this work. It ties together the research questions, findings, and implications into a coherent argument for why human-centered design must be at the center of how AI and cybersecurity systems are built and governed, grounded in existing scholarship and directed toward practical application.</div>
        <div class="chapter-topics">
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>Applied contribution:</strong> What this dissertation applies, synthesizes, and advances in practice. Consistent with the Purdue D.Tech. program's focus on addressing real-world industry gaps through applied research, this work draws on existing scholarship across AI, human-computer interaction, cybersecurity, accessibility, and organizational implementation to produce actionable guidance for technology professionals, organizations, and policymakers. The contribution is not a new theory - it is a more complete, applied understanding of a real and underexamined problem, organized in a way that practitioners can use.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>The core argument:</strong> AI and cybersecurity cannot be built well without centering the people who use them - and the people who are most at risk when those systems fail. That is not a design preference; it is an ethical obligation and a practical necessity.</div>
          <div class="chapter-topic"><div class="chapter-topic-dot"></div><strong>A call to action:</strong> The systems being built today are shaping how billions of people experience technology, security, and AI. The time to build human-centered design in is now - before these systems become so embedded that changing them is impractical.
            <div class="cta-grid">
              <div class="cta-item"><div class="cta-label">Designers &amp; Technologists</div><div class="cta-text">Include vulnerable populations in requirements, research, and testing from the start - not as an edge case, not in a later release.</div></div>
              <div class="cta-item"><div class="cta-label">Businesses &amp; Organizations</div><div class="cta-text">Established enterprises, startups, and AI-native companies alike: human-centered AI and cybersecurity is the foundation of trustworthy technology, not a feature layer. This research goes deeper than most organizations have yet examined. Use it.</div></div>
              <div class="cta-item"><div class="cta-label">NGOs</div><div class="cta-text">The obligation to implement AI thoughtfully is highest where the people you serve have the least power to recover from harm. This research provides a framework for better decisions, even under resource constraints.</div></div>
              <div class="cta-item"><div class="cta-label">Government Agencies</div><div class="cta-text">You serve the populations most concentrated in every vulnerable category this dissertation addresses. Human-centered implementation is an obligation to those people - build it in before deployment, not after harm is documented.</div></div>
              <div class="cta-item"><div class="cta-label">Policymakers &amp; Researchers</div><div class="cta-text">Close the gap between what law requires and what genuine protection demands. Build the evidence base. Fund the research. The field needs both.</div></div>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-top:.9rem;font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-sub);line-height:1.7;font-style:italic;">The people most affected by how these systems are designed must be visible in the design process. The scale of each organization differs. The obligation does not.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <details class="chapter-status-details"><summary>Research Status</summary><span class="chapter-status-text">Proposed, content pending research findings</span></details>
      </div>
    </div>

